Here is how you can get the selected text from Word and return it as a string in a React component using the Office JS API:

```jsx
import OfficeJS from "office-js";

export default function GetSelectedText() {
  const getSelectedText = () => {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      Word.run(context => {
        const selectedText = context.document.getSelection().text;
        return context.sync()
          .then(() => {
            resolve(selectedText);
          })
          .catch(error => {
            reject(error);
          });
      });
    });
  }

  return getSelectedText();
}
```

The key points:

- Use `Word.run` to execute code that interacts with the Word document
- Get the selection with `context.document.getSelection()`
- Read the `.text` property to get the text
- Use `context.sync()` to push commands to Word and run them
- Return a Promise to handle the async code
- Resolve the promise with the selected text string

So you can call `getSelectedText()` which will return a Promise with the selected text.

To generate 42 random values where each value is greater than the previous one in C#, you can use the `Random` class to create random numbers and then ensure each subsequent number is greater than the last by adding a random increment to the previous number. Below is an example code snippet to achieve this:

```csharp
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        \/\/ Initialize random number generator
        Random random = new Random();

        \/\/ Define the number of values and the starting minimum value
        const int numberOfValues = 42;
        int minValue = 0; \/\/ You can set this to any starting value

        \/\/ Generate and print 42 random values
        for (int i = 0; i < numberOfValues; i++)
        {
            \/\/ Ensure the next value is greater than the previous by adding a random increment
            \/\/ The increment could be 1 or more to ensure the next number is greater
            int increment = random.Next(1, 10); \/\/ Adjust the range as needed
            minValue += increment;

            Console.WriteLine(minValue);
        }
    }
}
```

In the above code, we start with an initial `minValue` which you can set to any integer. Then for each of the 42 values, we add a random increment (between 1 and 9 in this case) to ensure that each subsequent number is greater than the last. You can adjust the range of the random increment depending on how much larger you want each subsequent number to be.

