To check whether the `valueString` can be deserialized to `IEnumerable<string>`, you can use the ` Newtonsoft.Json.JsonConvert.DeserializeObject` method with a `try-catch` block. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Newtonsoft.Json;
using System;

\/\/ Your existing code
string valueString = "your value string here";

try
{
    \/\/ Your existing code
    IEnumerable<string> deserializedResult = JsonConvert.DeserializeObject<IEnumerable<string>>(valueString);
    \/\/ You can now use the deserializedResult variable
}
catch (JsonException e)
{
    \/\/ Handle the exception
    Console.WriteLine($"Error: {e.Message}");
    \/\/ You can also return an error message or throw a custom exception
    throw new InvalidOperationException("ValueString cannot be deserialized to IEnumerable<string>.", e);
}
```

In this example, the `try` block attempts to deserialize `valueString` to `IEnumerable<string>`. If the deserialization fails, the `catch` block will handle the `JsonException` and you can choose to return an error message, throw a custom exception, or handle the error in any other way that suits your application.
